{# Horizontal inline nav: one line of links separated by bullets, with an optional
   "you are here" item. Deliberately plain — it is a row of links, not a tab bar or a
   segmented control, because both callers use it for a handful of peer destinations
   that read as a sentence.

   Two shapes of nav, one component:
     - page switch (/places.html <-> /ecoregions.html) — mark the current page with
       `current: true`, which renders aria-current="page" and drops the link colour.
     - in-page jumps (collector detail) — plain fragment hrefs, nothing current.

   `items` is a list of { href, label, current? }. Callers build it inline and are
   expected to omit entries whose target section does not render, so an item never
   points at a heading that is not on the page. Falsy entries are skipped so a caller
   can write a conditional straight into the list literal.

   `label` is the nav's accessible name (aria-label on the <nav>) and is REQUIRED:
   the collector page has two navs (this one and .event-pagination), and an unnamed
   landmark is indistinguishable from the other in a screen reader's landmark list.

   Styles live in src/index.css, not a page stylesheet — src/entries/bee-header.ts
   imports it, so every page in _layouts/default.njk already has it and a shared
   component costs no extra request. #}

{% macro inlineNav(items, label) %}
<nav class="inline-nav" aria-label="{{ label }}">
  <ul>
    {%- for item in items -%}
    {%- if item -%}
    <li>
      {%- if item.current -%}
      <a href="{{ item.href }}" aria-current="page">{{ item.label }}</a>
      {%- else -%}
      <a href="{{ item.href }}">{{ item.label }}</a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </li>
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  </ul>
</nav>
{% endmacro %}
